.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
X.509 certificate chains as keys (RFC 6187), through GnuTLS.
.PP
A key of type x509v3\-ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp256, x509v3\-ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp384 or x509v3\-rsa2048\-sha256 is a chain of certificates, the end certificate first, with the signatures of the key in the end certificate: the blob (gf_key_from_blob) is the RFC 6187 public key format.  It is a key like any other to the transport and the authentication layer, and, like an OpenSSH certificate, only shows what its holder claims until the application has asked this module whether to believe it.
.PP
What is not here, on purpose: x509v3\-ssh\-rsa (its signature is SHA\-1) and x509v3\-ssh\-dss, the P\-521 curve, keys the policy would not take outside a certificate (RSA under 2048 bits), and any chain that GnuTLS finds to be signed with an algorithm it considers broken. Revocation is checked against the CRLs the application gives the trust store, and against the OCSP responses that came with the chain (stapled, as RFC 6187 provides).  Nothing is fetched from anywhere: a server gets its own responses by whatever means it has and attaches them (gf_key_with_x509_ocsp), and a client uses what it is shown.
.PP
Without GnuTLS (configure \-\-without\-gnutls) these keys are not available and every function here returns GF_ERR_UNAVAILABLE.
