The end certificate of KEY, and so on up the chain, as DER; the pointer is valid until the key is freed.  A key that is not an X.509 chain has none (0 certificates).
.TP
int \fBgf_key_with_x509\fP (const gf_key *key, const uint8_t *const *certs, const size_t *lens, size_t n, gf_key **out);
The key with the chain attached: KEY must have a private part, be an ECDSA P\-256, ECDSA P\-384 or RSA (at least 2048 bits) key, and be the key in the first certificate.  The certificates are DER, end certificate first and each issued by the next; at most 8.  The result presents the chain and signs with KEY's private part under the matching x509v3 algorithm.
.TP
int \fBgf_key_with_x509_ocsp\fP (const gf_key *key, const uint8_t *const *certs, const size_t *lens, size_t n, const uint8_t *const *ocsp, const size_t *ocsp_lens, size_t nocsp, gf_key **out);
As gf_key_with_x509, with OCSP responses (DER, at most 8) to staple to the chain.  The responses are sent as they are; a server that wants fresh ones makes a new key from time to time and offers that to new connections.
