void \fBgf_x509_trust_require_ocsp\fP (gf_x509_trust *t, int require);
With REQUIRE, a chain is good only if the end certificate comes with a good, fresh OCSP response from its issuer (default: it is not required, but one that is there is always checked).  A server that does not staple then fails the client; for a closed PKI whose servers all do.
.TP
int \fBgf_x509_verify\fP (const gf_x509_trust *t, const gf_key *key, int purpose, const char *name, uint64_t now, const char **why);
Does the trust store vouch for the chain of KEY, for PURPOSE, at NOW (seconds since 1970)?  GF_OK, or GF_ERR_HOSTKEY with *WHY (if not NULL) a short reason.  The checks, all of which must pass:
.IP \(bu 3
the chain leads from the end certificate, each issued by the next, to a CA in the store, none of them signed with MD5 or SHA\-1 and none of them a key weaker than 112 bits;
.IP \(bu 3
every certificate, the CA's too, is in its dates at NOW;
.IP \(bu 3
none is on a CRL of the store;
.IP \(bu 3
every stapled OCSP response that is about a certificate of the chain is well formed, signed (by the issuer, or by a responder certificate it issued for OCSP signing, in its dates) without SHA\-1 or MD5, current at NOW (thisUpdate not later than NOW, and NOW before nextUpdate; where there is no nextUpdate, within seven days of thisUpdate), and does not say "revoked" or "unknown".  A response that is not about any certificate of the chain is ignored; one that cannot be read, is not successful, or is not signed as above fails the chain, since a server that staples a broken response is misbehaving.  A response that is stale fails it too;
.IP \(bu 3
if a certificate has an extended key usage, it allows the purpose (the secure shell server or client OID of RFC 6187; "any" and the TLS server and client OIDs do not count);
.IP \(bu 3
the end certificate's key usage, if it has one, includes digitalSignature;
.IP \(bu 3
for GF_X509_SERVER with a NAME, one of the certificate's subject alternative names is that host: a dNSName, with a "*." wildcard for one label, or an iPAddress.  The common name is not looked at.  Without a NAME this is not checked, as for a user.
.PP
Which user the chain's subject is, is for the application: see gf_x509_subject and gf_x509_san.
